package types

// Package types holds Tack's type representation, a port of the Go
// compiler's package types: the basic types and what is known of them
// without a Table, and the Table (table.tck, Go's table.go), which gives
// every other type its ID, in the order the type checker adds them,
// Go's order, names them and answers what a type is made of, owns and
// holds, and how C lays it out (size.tck, Go's size.go). Every walk
// keeps a stack of its own, none recursing: along the structs and
// unions a type holds by value, a chain as long as its source makes it,
// and into a type's own parts, an element, a key, a parameter, which
// nest as deeply as the narrowings of nested switches on type
// parameters build them, not only as deeply as a type expression the
// parser bounds.
// Go's Bind, which makes a type parameter stand for a type while the
// backend lowers an instance, comes with the backend.
//
// A Type is an ID: a small integer that, for a composite type, a Table
// gives meaning to. Every distinct type has exactly one ID within a
// compilation, so == on two Types is type identity. The zero value,
// invalid, marks an expression that has not been checked or whose type
// could not be determined, so a node's type the type checker has not
// filled in holds invalid.
//
// The basic types have fixed IDs, numbered as Go's constants are, the
// same in every compilation: int64, string, bool, the signed integers
// int8 to int32, the unsigned uint8 to uint64, the target-width
// unsafe_size and unsafe_uintptr, untyped_int, any, error, allocator,
// float32, float64 and untyped_float, after invalid at 0. A Table's own
// types start at num_basic. Go's Type is a named int; Tack declares no
// named integer types, so here a Type is a struct wrapping its number,
// each basic type a global, and number gives the ID the --res dump
// writes.
//
// lookup maps a basic type's name as a program writes it, such as
// int64, to its Type; untyped_int and untyped_float have no such name.
// reserved says which names no declaration may take: a basic type's, and
// id, span and handle, the builtin types written with an element type.
// in_any, is_untyped, is_float, is_numeric, is_integer, is_unsigned,
// target_width, bits, portable_bits, max_bits and widens describe the
// basic types as Go's functions of those names do.
//
// It is a leaf package: it imports nothing of the compiler.
